[{:db/id 17592186314759 :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ y=5-4t \\\\ z=2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector parallel to the line that points in the direction of increasing $t$." :question/worked-solution "Write the position vector as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\begin{pmatrix}-2\\\\5\\\\\\frac17\\end{pmatrix}\n+t\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac13\\\\-4\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor any positive increment $h$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t+h)-\\mathbf r(t)=h\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac13\\\\-4\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\left(\\frac13,-4,2\\right)$ is parallel to the line and points in the direction of increasing $t$. Its negative $\\left(-\\frac13,4,-2\\right)$ is also parallel, but points in the direction of decreasing $t$ and does not satisfy the requested orientation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
